1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Binance's own Agent OS guardrail stops an agent from withdrawing funds. It does not stop it from losing money through bad trades.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:18,000
No position limits. No kill switch. That's the gap this project fills.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:28,000
This is a guardrail layer that sits in front of any trading agent. Every order gets checked before it ever reaches Binance.

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
A normal order. It passes every check and gets forwarded.

5
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Duplicate retried — dedup blocks it.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Oversized order (30k notional) — position cap blocks it.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:52,000
A buggy agent stuck in a decision loop fires fifteen orders in two seconds. Watch the rate limiter cut it off — first per symbol, then globally.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,700
Three consecutive losing trades trip the circuit breaker — blocked until a human manually resets it.

9
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:01,000
A manual kill switch halts everything instantly — independent of the breaker.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:18,000
This is infrastructure, not a strategy — it works underneath any agent.

11
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Five checks, one process: kill switch, circuit breaker, position cap, dedup, and rate limiter.

12
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Running live right now on Railway, with a real-time dashboard.

13
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Code, tests, and dashboard — all on GitHub. Link on screen.
